### A2 probe oracle (offline, DuckDB CLI, /tmp/sds_a2_*.sql|.out)
Ground truth via temp reconstructions (t18a=45431, t26c=1261, t30c=14655,
t31a=1524, t11d=95870 — all exact). Probe formulations tested:
- `USING SAMPLE 1% (system)`: FAILS — block sampling on clustered join
  keys returned 0 for mk×t11d (truth 3.2M). Unusable.
- `USING SAMPLE 1% (bernoulli)` on the large side: accurate (0.1–3.6%,
  7/7 correct decisions) but costs 41ms (mc) – 1.0s (ci full 36M scan).
- **sampled-temp-keys** (reservoir 512 rows from temp, join big table,
  scale by temp_rows/512): essentially exact — with-filter 0.7–8.6% at
  69–88ms (ci.note IN string filter forces 36M-row note column scan);
  filter-free 0.1–3.3% at 2–32ms (ci floor 32ms = movie_id column scan;
  mk 2ms). `COUNT(*) FILTER` variant still 86ms (note column projected).
- key-sampling from the SMALL side of base×base (9c cn-keys): FAILS
  under fanout skew (est 171K vs truth 1.15M) — heavy hitters missed.
**Verdict: accuracy PASS, economics FAIL.** Real per-query recoverable
exe (v15 CSVs): 11d +35.6, 9c +27.5, 18a +20.5, 30c +19.9, 31a +18.6,
26c +11.4 ms (134 ms over 6 queries). Accurate probes cost 22–86 ms per
terminal decision → net ≈ 0 or negative. Runtime sampling probes DROPPED.

### A3-lite (v16): relax terminal EXPLAIN gates instead (no sampling)
The existing engine-EXPLAIN fallback (~1–3 ms, batched, cached) is
accurate on these pairs because the engine sees exact temp stats:
measured est vs actual — 18a 163K/107K, 30c 53K/11.9K, 31a 5.5K/1.6K,
26c 2268/190K (the one underestimate), 11d 2.55M/3.2M. Two changes in
topdown_splitter.cpp PlanNext:
1. Engine-validated fallback gate `NumRelations() >= 4` → `>= 3`
   (the 3-relation {big, name, temp} terminals become eligible).
2. Rejected-pair cand_limit: max(temp_card, kPairEstFloor) →
   kSplitCardThreshold (1M). Expansion beyond the temp card is exactly
   what NB profitably does; the 1M abort bound still rejects 11d
   (est 2.55M). kPairEstFloor deleted; rejected_pairs no longer carries
   the temp cardinality.
