## Lever C diagnostics: what split shape does query-jit like? (2026-07-11)

Question (user, after rejecting B3): which split strategy gives TPDE +
template-cache faster jit and/or exe? Three diagnostics, no strategy code.

### Methodology
Debug build (AQP_DUMP_CACHE_KEYS is #ifndef NDEBUG, ir_to_llvm.cpp:8892).
Per-query runs of all 113 JOB queries under q_tpde + single-run-template
for BOTH splits, dumping one TSV per query
(SEQ / HIT / KEY / OPT / PLAN-serialization per qjit compile attempt):
  /tmp/keys_sds/*.tsv (113 files), /tmp/keys_nb/*.tsv (110 files —
  20a/20b/20c have NO NB qjit compile attempts at all: MARK/semi-heavy
  shapes fall back, so NB pays zero jit there).
C2/C3 attribution from the existing v17 SDS and NB reference CSVs
(canonical parser groups), matching CSV per-statement jit columns to
dump SEQ order (matched 107 SDS / 93 NB queries; rest skipped on
group-count mismatch).
NOTE: debug build may have poisoned shared lib/*.a — rebuild
build_release before the next measurement.

### C1: template-cache audit — reuse is a DEAD END
- Within-run template hits: SDS **0/534**, NB **0/539**. ZERO for both.
  NB's jit advantage is NOT template reuse — prior hypothesis WRONG.
- Why: serialized plan templates embed table names (`keyword#1B`,
  `temp1#1T`), column names, concrete types (3=int, 7=string) and hash
  layouts (`H{1,8,16|1.0.3@8}`). Every sub-statement in a run differs.
- Name-agnostic canonicalization (strip table/col names, keep
  shape+types): within-run hits rise only ~2% (SDS) / 0% (NB) — payload
  column sets differ per split step even when the join shape matches.
- Cross-QUERY equivalence classes (illegal to exploit under single-run
  semantics, measured for completeness): SDS 534→312, NB 539→416.
⇒ Any "template-normal-form" split design is dead; the cache key is not
the lever.

### C2: jit-time attribution — the gap is per-SUB-query, not finals
- Final-query compiles are EQUAL: SDS 1.137 vs NB 1.116 ms/final
  (suite: 128 vs 126 ms) — even though NB finals are BIGGER.
- Whole +85 ms jit gap = sub-query statements:
  SDS 0.703 ms/stmt x 412 stmts vs NB 0.404 ms/stmt x 512 stmts.
  SDS compiles FEWER but each costs 1.7x more.

### C3: per-sub jit scales with statement size (#relations)
  ms/stmt by relation count (matched CSV<->dump):
    rel:   2      3      4      5      6      7
    SDS  0.522  0.762  1.391  1.039  2.690  2.638
    NB   0.359  0.528  0.732  0.907   —      —
- Sub-statement size histograms: SDS mean 2.55 rel (55 stmts >=4 rel);
  NB mean 2.24 rel (13 stmts >=4 rel). NB never emits >5-rel subs.
- NB finals mean 3.68 rel (max 13) vs SDS finals 3.03 — yet equal cost
  (finals are compiled once, dominated by fixed overhead).
- UNEXPLAINED residual at equal size: 2-rel SDS 0.522 vs NB 0.359
  ms/stmt (1.45x). Suspects: (a) spec-wait charged into jit column via
  ConsumeSpecWaitUs, (b) temp payload width (SDS temps carry wider
  target lists), (c) storage-plan scan-layer differences.
  → Lever C TODO 1.

### Conclusion (feeds CLAUDE.md §Lever C)
Query-jit "likes" MANY SMALL statements over few large ones: per-stmt
compile cost grows superlinearly with relation count, while per-stmt
fixed cost is small; template reuse contributes nothing either way.
This validates the user's hybrid: keep SDS middleware (IR-DP order,
exact-card feedback, cross-query prep, bg-compile, zero fallbacks; MW
0.06) but emit NB-granularity boundaries — split at each hash-join
BUILD side of the IR-DP join tree (binary temp x base steps; SEMI/MARK
not split; 1M abort guard; never materialize big x temp probes per
Lever A). Expected: jit 0.42→~0.34, MW stays 0.06, exe >= NB
⇒ target total ~4.95–5.05.

### Lever C TODO 1: 2-rel jit residual — ROOT CAUSE FOUND (2026-07-11)
Question: why does an SDS 2-rel sub cost 0.522 ms jit vs NB 0.359?
Suspects (a) spec-wait, (b) temp payload width, (c) storage-plan layer:
ALL DEAD. Real cause: **the qjit pending-IR fast path is strategy-gated
to NODE_BASED** (ir_query_splitter.cpp:2727/:2800-2804). NB subs run
ExecuteSQLandCreateTempTable's fast path (TakePlan + PrepareFromPlan +
AnnotateBuildSides, duckdb_adapter.cpp:1442-1476 — no parse, no
optimize, no IR re-extraction); SDS subs fall through to
PrepareWithQueryJitAnalysis (:3007) = ParseSQL + full Optimize +
RebuildTempTableIndices + ConvertPlanToIR + PrepareFromPlan, ALL inside
the jit CSV column.

Evidence (release build, per-query 15-iter runs, warm iters 5-14):
1. Suspect elimination from matched v17/NB CSVs x key dumps (379/411
   sub-stmts): pos-0 subs ~0.01 ms in BOTH (bg-compile HIT works;
   spec-wait dead — also dead by code path: AddSpecWaitTime only fires
   from spec-jit, which is off). Structure at 2-rel near-identical:
   cols 6.7 vs 7.1, hash payload 1.59 vs 1.98 (NB WIDER), temp scans
   0.51 vs 0.64 (NB MORE) — payload/scan-layer dead. Residual persists
   within structure class: base-only 0.348 vs 0.196, with-temp 0.725
   vs 0.481.
2. AQP_QJIT_PASS2_TRACE on SDS 8a (warm): per sub total 0.29-0.39 ms
   of which parse 0.09-0.16 + optimize 0.12-0.18 = 80-90%; conv ~0.03,
   PrepareFromPlan ~0.02.
3. Ablation via existing AQP_NO_FAST_PATH on NB 8a — per-sub jit ms:
     SDS (slow path): 0.74, 0.99, 0.97, 1.00          final 0.70
     NB fast        : 0.49, 0.56, 0.41, 0.57, 0.44    final 0.78
     NB nofast      : 0.77, 0.91, 0.63, 1.03, 0.73    final 0.68
   NB-nofast reproduces SDS per-sub costs almost exactly; finals equal
   everywhere — both strategies use the slow path for finals, which is
   exactly C2's "finals equal" observation.

Suite-level estimate: 412 SDS subs x ~0.25-0.35 ms parse+opt+conv
= 100-140 ms — the whole +85 ms jit gap is this fast-path asymmetry,
not statement size per se (size only scales the parse+opt cost).

Bonus observation (common cost, NOT residual): with-temp 2-rel subs
cost ~2x base-only in BOTH strategies (SDS 0.73/0.35, NB 0.48/0.20)
— temp-source resolution/chunk-scan annotate; possible separate lever.

Why SDS can't just flip the gate: the fast path needs the engine
logical plan matching the sub-IR. NB has it (the engine split the
optimized plan); SDS only has sub-IR + generated SQL — and SDS RELIES
on the engine's full Optimize for join order INSIDE multi-relation
clusters. Options feed Lever C TODO 2 (hybrid design): binary
temp x base subs have trivial join order -> a small closed IR->logical-
plan constructor makes the fast path available to the hybrid.

### Lever C TODO 2 pre-design verification (2026-07-11, source-confirmed)
All constructor-feasibility assumptions verified read-only (middleware +
patched DuckDB tree), no blockers:
1. PrepareFromPlan (duckdb client_context.cpp:1244-1268) accepts a
   hand-built LogicalOperator + names/types, skips the optimizer, goes
   straight to PhysicalPlanGenerator. Plan must be type-resolved
   (ResolveOperatorTypes).
2. Temps are in-memory ColumnDataCollections (temp_collections_,
   replacement scan "scan_temp_collection"/"scan_qjit_temp",
   duckdb_adapter.cpp:335-391). LogicalColumnDataGet is directly
   constructible over an existing CDC — NB already does this for spec
   chunks (node_based_splitter.cpp:400-410) = in-tree precedent.
3. Base-table LogicalGet: no one-call helper; route = Binder::
   CreateBinder(context) + bind table ref, then set column_ids +
   ResolveOperatorTypes. Filter pushdown WITHOUT full Optimize: patched
   public Optimizer::FilterOptimize (optimizer.cpp:1265) runs only
   ExpressionRewriter/FilterPullup/FilterPushdown → populates
   LogicalGet::table_filters (zone maps) on a constructed plan; no
   hand-built TableFilterSet needed. TableFilter classes cover =,<,>,
   IN, IS (NOT) NULL, ExpressionFilter for the rest.
4. Hash join build side = RIGHT child, fixed (plan_comparison_join.
   cpp:22-63); PrepareFromPlan path runs NO BuildProbeSideOptimizer →
   constructor orders children itself (build = smaller side, known
   EXACTLY from temp feedback). estimated_cardinality used for join
   type thresholds → set it from our exact/DP cards.
5. LogicalComparisonJoin needs JoinCondition of BoundColumnRefExpression
   with correct ColumnBindings + projection maps.
6. qjit fast-path IR contract: temps MUST be ChunkNodes in the IR
   (BuildExecutionSteps sets source_is_temp only for ChunkNode;
   positional column resolution from qjit_temps_). SDS's current
   BuildSubIRForCluster emits plain scans for temps (fk_based_splitter.
   cpp:959-1294) → the hybrid sub-IR builder must emit ChunkNodes for
   temp sides. AnnotateBuildSides(include_chunk_scans=true) +
   AnalyzeQueryJit then work as for NB.
User decisions locked (2026-07-11): constructor fallback = per-sub SQL
round trip when shape unsupported; exe hypothesis accepted as
measure-and-revert risk (kill switch A/B).

## Lever C TODO 3: Hybrid SDS v18 — IMPLEMENTED (2026-07-11, v18)

Design per user-approved plan (binary NB-granularity boundaries + closed
IR→DuckDB-logical-plan constructor for the qjit pending-IR fast path).

### What changed
- Phase 0 kill switches (topdown_splitter.h): `AQP_TD_V17` = full v17
  restore (growth block on, ctor off, fast-path gate NB-only);
  `AQP_TD_NO_PLANCTOR` = binary boundaries but no ctor/fast path.
- Phase 1 (topdown_splitter.cpp): the multi-relation GROWTH BLOCK
  (grow the harvested pair up the optimal join tree) is now v17-only —
  default v18 emits the bottom-level binary pair from visit_pairs.
  All gates unchanged (big×big, big×temp fanout, 1M threshold, EXPLAIN
  validation, component fallback → those groups keep the SQL path).
- Phase 2 (SplitIR post-processing, v18 only; shared fk_based_splitter
  untouched): `PushFilterConjunctsToScans` + `WrapTempScansAsChunks`
  (temp scans → SimplestChunk, required by qjit source_is_temp) +
  `RenumberRootProjection` + leaf estimated_cardinality stamping from
  the DP's `table_card_` (exact for temps).
- Phase 3 (duckdb_adapter.cpp `TryBuildBinaryPlanFromIR` :3317): v1
  accepted shape = projection over ONE inner equi-join of two
  scan/chunk leaves with conjunctive constant filters. Base leaf:
  Binder bind → LogicalGet + patched `Optimizer::FilterOptimize` for
  table_filters (zone-map parity). Temp leaf: LogicalColumnDataGet
  over the existing CDC. Join: LogicalComparisonJoin, RIGHT child =
  build = smaller side by our exact/DP cards. Reject → per-sub SQL
  round trip (v17 behavior). Hooked INSIDE the fast path; plan output
  names derived from IR target_list (ComputeColumnAlias mirror).
- Phase 4 (ir_query_splitter.cpp): `SetQjitPendingIR` un-gated for
  TOP_DOWN (use_engine_plan=false → ctor path), subject to switches.

### Bugs found & fixed during verification
1. **Projection-index-0 collision**: BuildSubIRForCluster hardcodes the
   root projection's table index to 0; qjit's ThroughProj remaps attrs
   of ANY matching index → base table 0 in the cluster got its attrs
   misrouted (dtype mismatch, e.g. 8a temp4 expected INT32 where the
   plan had VARCHAR aka_name.name). BuildQjitOutputDescs' type
   cross-check masked it as a compile reject; could have been silent
   wrong data on coincidental type match. Fix: `RenumberRootProjection`
   rebuilds the sub-IR root SimplestProjection with index =
   max(leaf table index)+1 (donor ctor; SQL rendering unaffected).
2. **Wrong hash-join build side**: sub-IR leaves carry
   estimated_cardinality=0 (FilterOptimize-only IR), so the ctor's
   smaller-right selection defaulted — 1a temp1 built on movie_info_idx
   (1.38M) and probed filtered info_type (1 row). Fix: stamp
   `table_card_` onto sub-IR scan/chunk leaves in SplitIR.
Also restored a corrupted `flag_suffix` line in
measure_breakdown_time_job.sh (unconditional `+="${compile_mode}"`
would have broken all CSV names).

### Constructor accept rate (suite, AQP_QJIT_FAST_TRACE)
343 fast-path attempts → 263 constructed+compiled (77%); 80 rejects all
`leaf-not-scan` = mark-join units (7*/9*/14*/22*/25*/28*–31*/33*),
the designed v1 SQL fallback; ZERO post-construction compile failures.
Constructed plans verified: table_filters present, build side correct.

### Verification & measurement (2026-07-11)
- Correctness: 113/113 vs node-based golden, BOTH configs (topdown
  no-jit; topdown query tpde single-run-template). All measurement runs
  1695/1695.
