## NB parity levers: SDS-idea transfer to node-based (2026-07-11/12, CLOSED)

User question post-v18: can NB be sped up with SDS's infrastructure
ideas WITHOUT changing its split algorithm (e.g. MW reduction)?

### Audit (code-verified)
Cross-query prep AND first-sub bg-compile ALREADY EXIST for NB — they
originated there (Track B §7.2): `IRQuerySplitter::PrepareNextQuery`
(ir_query_splitter.cpp:452) runs parse + FilterOptimize +
MiddleOptimize + QuerySplit iteration 1 + first-sub
SpeculativeQueryJitCompile on a bg Connection; dispatched for
NODE_BASED at aqp_middleware.cpp:597-605, gate `jit_cache >= 1`
(includes single-run-template). The NB 5.30 baseline already includes
both; SDS inherited the pattern from NB. NB's remaining MW 0.38 =
per-iteration ReOptimizeIR re-plans (exact-temp-card DP re-runs,
inherently sequential) + IR→SQL→parse round trip per iteration.

### B2 optimizer trim for NB — TESTED, NULL RESULT, REVERTED (2026-07-12)
Only transferable piece: NB's ReOptimizeIR runs the FULL ~35-pass
Optimize() (duckdb_adapter.cpp:930, split flag temporarily off) once
per iteration in the MW column; finals pay it again in jit.
RunOptimizer honors disabled_optimizers (optimizer.cpp:94-99) and the
setting is DBConfig-GLOBAL (settings.hpp DisabledOptimizersSetting:
SetGlobal only) → one SET also covers the bg-prep connection.
Implemented the SDS 20-pass SET in the NodeBasedSplitter ctor (kill
switch AQP_NB_NO_OPTSET) and verified/measured:
- Correctness: 113/113 vs NB golden, BOTH configs (node-based none;
  node-based query tpde single-run-template); measurement runs
  1695/1695.
- Split identity: per-row column-count structure of the breakdown CSVs
  IDENTICAL suite-wide between trim ON and OFF (and vs golden) — the
  20 passes are no-ops for full JOB queries too; split unchanged.
- Same-session A/B (canonical parser, jit/MW/exe/total):
  ON  0.35 / 0.38 / 4.77 / 5.50
  OFF 0.35 / 0.39 / 4.75 / 5.49
  → jit ±0, MW −10 ms (noise floor), exe +20 ms (noise; plans
  identical). Session drift note: this session measures NB ~5.49-5.50
  vs the historical 5.30; the back-to-back A/B is the valid comparison.
- Why null vs SDS's −22 ms: SDS's ~500 sub-statements each paid the
  full Prepare pipeline (the trimmed passes = measurable slice); NB
  subs use the TakePlan fast path (no Optimize at all), so only ~600
  re-plans/finals pay it, and there JOIN_ORDER DP dominates so
  completely that the trimmed tree-walk passes are negligible.
REVERTED per user decision 2026-07-12 (clean tree; avoid a
JOB-only-validated global optimizer disable). Evidence CSVs kept:
`job_result/duckdb_node-based_query_none_jitcache_single_run_template_`
`fctpde_breakdown_time_log_nbtrim{,_ab_off}.csv`.

**Conclusion: NB has no remaining SDS-transferable overhead lever.**
Its MW floor is the sequential exact-card re-plan loop — the price of
its exe advantage; the fair same-infrastructure comparison already
holds (both strategies share prep/bg-compile/fast-path machinery).

